---
/* Three levels, one real object.
   Research question: why do careful sources disagree about how many articles
   the magazine holds? Answer, shown rather than argued: the same page counts
   as 1, 1 or 3 depending on the level you count at. The example is located
   from the corpus (the largest multi-member editorial unit), not named by
   hand, so it stays correct if the corpus is re-frozen. */
import j from '../data/janghan.json';

const s = j.symposium;
const LX = 196;                       // diagram left edge
const BOXW = 540;
const ROW3_X = LX + 44;
const rowY = { entry: 26, unit: 116, items: 212 };
const ITEMH = 50, ITEMGAP = 10;
const items = s ? s.answers.map((a, i) => ({ ...a, y: rowY.items + i * (ITEMH + ITEMGAP) })) : [];
const H = items.length ? items[items.length - 1].y + ITEMH + 16 : 320;

const levels = [
  { y: rowY.entry, h: 40, label: 'Bibliographic entry', badge: `${s?.bib_entries ?? 1} entry`,
    note: 'what an external catalogue records' },
  { y: rowY.unit, h: 48, label: 'Editorial unit', badge: '1 unit',
    note: 'what the magazine itself presents' },
  { y: rowY.items, h: ITEMH, label: 'Textual item', badge: `${s?.item_count ?? 3} items`,
    note: 'each continuous signed text' },
];
---
<figure id="fig-levels">
  <div class="fig-head">
    <span class="fig-no">Figure 2 · Three levels</span>
    <p class="fig-title">
      One page of 長恨, counted three ways — this is why the witnesses disagree
    </p>
  </div>

  {s && (
    <svg viewBox={`0 0 760 ${H}`} role="img"
      aria-label={`Diagram. One bibliographic entry, ${s.bib_label}, corresponds to one editorial unit — the prompt ${s.prompt} — which contains ${s.item_count} separate signed textual items: ${s.answers.map((a) => `${a.title}, signed ${a.byline}`).join('; ')}. A catalogue counting the answers separately and a magazine presenting them as one feature both give an honest but different count.`}>

      {levels.map((L) => (
        <>
          <text x="0" y={L.y + 17} font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink)">
            {L.label}
          </text>
          <text x="0" y={L.y + 34} font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--faint)">
            {L.note}
          </text>
          <text x="0" y={L.y + 52} font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1"
            fill="var(--b4)">{L.badge.toUpperCase()}</text>
        </>
      ))}

      {/* entry → unit */}
      <line x1={LX + 22} y1={rowY.entry + 40} x2={LX + 22} y2={rowY.unit}
        stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.5" />
      {/* unit → items: spine plus one stub per item */}
      <line x1={LX + 22} y1={rowY.unit + 48} x2={LX + 22} y2={items[items.length - 1].y + ITEMH / 2}
        stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.5" />
      {items.map((it) => (
        <line x1={LX + 22} y1={it.y + ITEMH / 2} x2={ROW3_X} y2={it.y + ITEMH / 2}
          stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.5" />
      ))}

      {/* bibliographic entry */}
      <rect x={LX} y={rowY.entry} width={BOXW} height="40" rx="4"
        fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--b4)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3" />
      <text x={LX + 16} y={rowY.entry + 25} font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12.5" fill="var(--b4)">
        {s.bib_label}
      </text>
      <text x={LX + 86} y={rowY.entry + 25} font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="13" fill="var(--muted)">
        one record in the Yonsei catalogue
      </text>

      {/* editorial unit */}
      <rect x={LX} y={rowY.unit} width={BOXW} height="48" rx="4"
        fill="var(--card)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" />
      <text x={LX + 16} y={rowY.unit + 21} font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="13.5"
        font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink)" lang="ko">
        {s.prompt}
      </text>
      <text x={LX + 16} y={rowY.unit + 38} font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--faint)">
        one editorial prompt, printed as a single feature
      </text>

      {/* textual items */}
      {items.map((it) => (
        <>
          <rect x={ROW3_X} y={it.y} width={BOXW - 44} height={ITEMH} rx="4"
            fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1" />
          <text x={ROW3_X + 14} y={it.y + 21} font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="13" fill="var(--ink)" lang="ko">
            {it.title}
          </text>
          <text x={ROW3_X + 14} y={it.y + 38} font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--muted)" lang="ko">
            signed {it.byline}
          </text>
        </>
      ))}
    </svg>
  )}

  <figcaption>
    A real feature from 창간호: the prompt <span lang="ko">{s?.prompt}</span> (“if I were a customer”), which
    the magazine prints as one item and three women answer over their own signatures. The Yonsei catalogue
    records it as {s?.bib_entries} entry; this edition records {s?.item_count} textual items inside
    1 editorial unit. Both counts are correct at their own level, and neither is wrong — which is precisely
    why the totals in Figure 1 can never be reconciled into a single number. Across both issues
    {j.nested_items} texts sit nested inside a parent unit this way, as prompt answers, poems printed within
    other articles, or serial parts. A byline recorded with a queried reading is shown as recorded.
  </figcaption>

  {s && (
    <details class="chart-table">
      <summary>Data table</summary>
      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr>
            <th scope="col">Level</th><th scope="col">Identifier</th>
            <th scope="col">Title / byline</th><th scope="col">Count here</th>
          </tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bibliographic entry</td><td>{s.bib_label}</td><td>Catalogued as one record</td><td class="num">{s.bib_entries}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Editorial unit</td><td>{s.unit_id}</td><td lang="ko">{s.prompt}</td><td class="num">1</td></tr>
            {s.answers.map((a) => (
              <tr><td>Textual item</td><td>—</td><td lang="ko">{a.title} · {a.byline}</td><td class="num">1</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
  )}
</figure>
